<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">All Blacks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as New Zealand national rugby union team</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The All Blacks are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s national rugby union team, named for their black kit and known for performing the haka before matches. Since their first international tour in 1884 they have become one of the most successful teams in any sport, winning the Rugby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> in 1987, 2011 and 2015. Rugby and the team occupy a central place in New Zealand's national identity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Rugby union was introduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> by Charles Monro in 1870, who had discovered it while studying in Finchley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The first recorded game took place in May 1870 at Nelson, between the Nelson rugby club and Nelson College. The first provincial union, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, was formed in 1879. In 1884 the first New Zealand team to travel overseas toured New South Wales and won all eight games. The New Zealand Rugby Football Union was formed in 1892 by most of the provincial unions, and the first officially sanctioned New Zealand side toured New South Wales in 1893, winning nine of ten matches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The team's first true test match was against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> on 15 August 1903 at the Sydney Cricket Ground, before over 30,000 spectators, and ended in a 22–3 victory. The first home test, against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> side, was played in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> in 1904. A 34-game tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1905 included five tests and brought one defeat, the team's first test loss, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. By then the side wore all black except for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> fern, and the name All Blacks dates from this tour. International rugby was suspended during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and a New Zealand Services team played in the King's Cup.</p><p>For the 1960 tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, South African authorities insisted that Maori players be excluded, and the controversy led the New Zealand Rugby Union to refuse any tour of the country for ten years, until 1970, when Maori players were accepted as honorary whites. New Zealand hosted and won the first Rugby World Cup in 1987, beating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> 29–9 in the final. In 1996 under coach John Hart and captain Sean Fitzpatrick the team won a test series in South Africa for the first time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The All Blacks compete with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in the Rugby Championship, which grew from the Tri-Nations when Argentina joined in 2012. They won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> in 1987, 2011 and 2015, the first country to retain it, and completed Grand Slam tours of the four Home Nations in 1978, 2005, 2008 and 2010. The team performs a haka before every match, traditionally Te Rauparaha's Ka Mate, with Kapa o Pango also performed since 2005. The current jersey is entirely black except for sponsors' logos and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> fern, but for the 1884 tour the team wore dark blue with a gold fern.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Zealand national rugby union team” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In culture and sport, the mountaineer Sir Edmund Hillary, the <b>All Blacks</b> rugby team and the films of Peter Jackson made the country widely known, and Jacinda Ardern's response to the Christchurch mosque shootings of 2019 drew worldwide attention.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Mate&quot; became the best-known haka in New Zealand and abroad because a choreographed, synchronised version has been performed before test matches by the <b>All Blacks</b>, New Zealand's rugby union team, and by the Kiwis, its rugby league team, until 2013, when the Kiwis took up &quot;Te Iwi Kiwi&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ka-mate">Ka Mate</a></li><li><blockquote>…2017 the New Zealand Rugby Union, in a campaign led by the police constable and former All Black Glen Osborne, urged <b>All Blacks</b> fans to adopt the song as a rallying chant to out-sing the supporters of the British and Irish Lions during their tour of New Zealand.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tutira-mai-nga-iwi">Tūtira Mai Ngā Iwi</a></li></ul></section>
